{"verdict": "reject","reasons": [{"check": "centroid","value": "verdict on_target, offset 4.459 arcsec err 4.216 sigma 1.058","why": "difference imaging puts the dip on the target, ruling out a neighbour (plausible_within_1px 0, closest could at 25.2 arcsec)","check": "shape and size","value": "shape v_sigma -5.212, rp_rearth 1.767, rp_over_rstar 0.08182","why": "flat-bottomed transit and planet-sized ratio argue against stellar eclipse, but size alone cannot separate a blended EB when other signs favour a binary","check": "secondary","value": "snr 34.32, phase 0.6697, depth_ppm 5716, depth_over_dip 0.8724, best_brightening_snr 23.71, secondary_over_bump 1.447","why": "a deep second dip at ~0.67 phase with amplitude almost equal to the primary transit is the eclipse of a stellar companion, not a planetary occultation","check": "phase_curve and density","value": "phase_curve amplitude_ppm 8278 ratio_to_depth 1.256 chi2 1812 dof 9, duration ratio 1.73 (observed 0.998 h vs central 0.5769 h), fit_b 0.9012, star_density_penalty 828.9","why": "large out-of-eclipse modulation and duration far longer than a central transit for this M dwarf indicate a grazing close binary with ellipsoidal/spot modulation, inconsistent with a planet","check": "rotation coincidence","value": "rotation_d 0.229, signal_over_rotation 2.996052401746725, amplitude_ppm 20225, false_alarm 2.20438e-196","why": "period is exactly 3x the strong 0.229-d spot period and sectors show huge variability; combined with the secondary and long V-shaped grazing fit it favours a synchronised stellar binary over chance alignment","check": "xgboost planet_rank","value": "0.09","why": "low planet-like score is expected for an EB with a flat dip and supports binary interpretation","check": "odd_even","value": "diff_sigma 1.001","why": "no odd/even difference does not favour an EB at 2x period, but EB at this period can be identical primary/secondaries as observed; also shape V can be grazing","check": "neighbours","value": "could_cause 30 but plausible_within_1px 0, could_cause_within_1px 0","why": "no star within one TESS pixel can cause the dip even at 25% depth, so EB must be on the target or fully diluted; centroid rules out off-target","check": "sector_depths sector_depths_by_source","value": "verdict changes_within_a_source, SPOC 2-min p_constant 0.0042752611434842495, depth_change_pct 73.14 (4232 to 8290 ppm)","why": "depth changes within the same product, not calibration, expected when stellar active variability or spot crossings modulate a grazing EB depth, not a stable planet","check": "Gaia binary flags","value": "ruwe 8.804, ipd_multi_peak 3, excess_noise_sig 1096","why": "very large Gaia astrometric noise indicates an unresolved binary, supporting the EB interpretation"}],
 "open_questions": [],
 "public_summary": "The dip looks like a small flat-bottomed transit but shows a second dip as deep as the first and strong brightness changes outside transits, consistent with a grazing stellar eclipse rather than a planet. The period is exactly three times the star's 0.23-day spin and the Gaia data show the star is a binary, matching the long duration and 73% depth changes. The signal is on the target, so it is an eclipsing M-dwarf binary, not a planet."}
